            <ArticleTitle>Progesterone skin test in severe attacks of angioedema during menstrual cycle.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>375-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Progesterone hypersensitivity (PH) is a rare clinical condition that displays hypersensitivity to endogenous or exogenous progesterone. It is characterized by cyclic dermatologic manifestations at the end of the luteal phase that disappear some days after menses. We present a case of 24-year-old woman showing severe angioedema attacks occurring from the first day of her menstruation and continuing for 4-5 days and having positive progesterone intradermal test (IDT). To our knowledge, there is no case in the literature which is coupled with PH isolated angioedema attacks. In this case report we will discuss diagnostic value of progesterone IDT.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
